{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"3978","artifact_type":"feature-context","name":"plan/feature-context-primary-quota-403-retryable.md@sha256:f3056914dc2329a05c8103acaad2cf6e7e3b37ad7ae17fea0567adf15598091f"},"owner_reference":"3978","content":"# Feature Context: Primary-quota 403 rate limit classified retryable\n\n## Document Metadata\n\n- **Generated**: 2026-09-28\n- **Input Form**: artifact (the composed feature request for backlog item #3978: groomed sections, acceptance criteria, Impact Radius, feasibility)\n- **Source**: backlog item #3978, \"fix: Primary-quota 403 rate limit is classified non-retryable\"\n- **Status**: DISCOVERY_COMPLETE\n- **Mode**: auto. No questions were put to the user. Open questions are recorded below with a resolution channel.\n\n---\n\n## Original Request\n\n> A REST 403 for the primary rate limit ('API rate limit exceeded', RateLimitExceededException, often without a Retry-After header) is classified NON_RETRYABLE by sync_state._classify_github_exception. Sync then goes OFFLINE and MCP responses report retryable: false while the limit is only temporary. Found by Codex on #3968 (PRRT_kwDOQGimCs6mbw5i); the behaviour predates #3968.\n>\n> User-provided context: classify RateLimitExceededException as retryable before the generic 403 rule, and take the delay from x-ratelimit-reset when Retry-After is absent.\n\nThe orchestrator scope note on the item adds one more requirement. The x-ratelimit-reset fallback must live in the shared delay parsing that both the REST classifier path and the GraphQL `GitHubRateLimitedError` path call (Acceptance Criterion 9). `sync_state` must not import `gh_client`.\n\n---\n\n## Core Intent Analysis\n\n### WHO (Target Users)\n\n1. **Agents calling dh backlog MCP tools** (`backlog_list`, `backlog_view`, `backlog_groom`, `sync_now` and the others). They branch on the `retryable` / `retry_after` fields of an error response to decide whether to wait and retry or to stop and report a hard failure. Evidence: `backlog_core/server.py:308-323` (`_failure_verdict`).\n2. **The background sync loop** (`backlog_core/sync_engine.py`) and every operator or agent who reads `sync_status`. OFFLINE means \"needs a human or a `sync_now` call\" (`sync_engine.py:8`, `:213-216`).\n3. **GitHub backend read/write paths** (`backends/github_backend.py`, `gh_client.py`). They wrap failures in `BackendUnavailableError(retryable=...)` using the same classifier.\n4. **Heavy-traffic users of the GitHub backend.** This covers live e2e lanes, milestone dispatch that fans out many sessions, and large grooming waves: the workloads that actually burn through the hourly primary quota. The item came out of the live e2e / GitHub robustness session (#3968, #3969, #3975).\n\n### WHAT (Desired Outcome)\n\nWhen GitHub refuses a request because the **primary** (hourly) quota is used up:\n\n- The failure is reported as **temporary** (retryable), not as a permanent permission or auth failure.\n- Callers get a **useful wait hint** that reflects when GitHub says the quota resets, even when GitHub sends no `Retry-After` header.\n- Background sync **keeps retrying** instead of parking in OFFLINE. It waits long enough that the retry does not hit the same exhausted quota.\n- The REST and GraphQL paths behave **the same way** for the same condition.\n- Genuine permission-denied 403s (missing scope, forbidden resource, environment-wide GraphQL refusal) **stay non-retryable**, and secondary-rate-limit handling does not regress.\n\n### WHEN (Trigger Conditions)\n\n- A REST call answers HTTP 403 with GitHub's \"API rate limit exceeded\u2026\" message and `x-ratelimit-remaining: 0`, usually with no `Retry-After`. PyGithub raises this as `RateLimitExceededException` (`.venv/.../github/Requester.py:954-955`, `isPrimaryRateLimitError` at `:971-976`: message starts with \"api rate limit exceeded\").\n- A GraphQL call is refused for the primary limit, with the quota headers present and no `Retry-After`. It is raised as `GitHubRateLimitedError` (`gh_client.py:965-967`).\n- Primary-limit responses can also arrive as HTTP **429**. GitHub's REST rate-limit docs say \"you will receive a 403 or 429 response\". A 429 is already classified RETRYABLE (`sync_state.py:377-378`), but it gets no delay hint without `Retry-After` (`sync_state.py:450-454`). See Q4.\n\n### WHY (Problem Being Solved)\n\n- **Wrong verdict:** today a primary-limit 403 with no `Retry-After` and no \"secondary rate limit\" wording falls through to `NON_RETRYABLE` (`sync_state.py:371-376`). A self-healing condition gets treated as permanent.\n- **Sync is stranded:** `_attempt_sync` sets `SyncStatus.OFFLINE` on NON_RETRYABLE (`sync_engine.py:205-216`). Sync then stops until someone calls `sync_now`, even though the quota would have reset without intervention.\n- **Agents are misled:** the MCP response says `retryable: false` with no `retry_after` (`server.py:308-323`), so agent callers give up on work they could finish after the reset.\n- **Delay information is thrown away:** GitHub documents `x-ratelimit-reset` (UTC epoch seconds) as the time to wait until for primary limits. The code reads only `Retry-After` (`sync_state.py:412-428`, `:431-455`; GraphQL path `gh_client.py:967`), so even the correctly classified GraphQL path has `retry_after=None` for a primary limit.\n\n---\n\n## Codebase Research\n\n### Similar Patterns Found\n\n#### Pattern 1: GraphQL rate-limit detector (primary + secondary)\n\n- **Location**: `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/gh_client.py:892-916` (`_is_rate_limited`)\n- **Relevance**: This detector already recognises a primary limit correctly: `isinstance(exc, RateLimitExceededException)`, a GraphQL `RATE_LIMITED` errors entry, or `x-ratelimit-remaining == \"0\"` on a 400/403/429 (`_RATE_LIMIT_REFUSAL_STATUSES`, `gh_client.py:106-107`). It is the codebase's existing definition of what \"rate limited\" means.\n- **Reusable**: the detection rules as precedent. The rules themselves cannot be imported into `sync_state`, because `gh_client` imports `sync_state` (`gh_client.py:76`) and the reverse is disallowed.\n\n#### Pattern 2: Secondary-rate-limit retryable branch (#3968)\n\n- **Location**: `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/sync_state.py:330-333` (`_SECONDARY_RATE_LIMIT_MARKER`), `:371-376` (403 branch)\n- **Relevance**: the closest precedent for this change. A 403 is reclassified RETRYABLE from a message marker or a `Retry-After` header, and a plain 403 stays NON_RETRYABLE. The new behaviour extends the same 403 arm.\n- **Reusable**: `_github_exception_message_text` (`sync_state.py:336-348`), which already duplicates `gh_client`'s message unwrapping to respect the import direction. That duplication rationale is documented in the function's docstring.\n\n#### Pattern 3: Shared delay hint parser used by REST, GraphQL, sync and MCP\n\n- **Location**: `sync_state.py:412-428` (`parse_retry_after_header`), `:431-455` (`retry_after_seconds`). Consumers are `sync_engine.py:221,259`, `server.py:321-322`, and `gh_client.py:967`.\n- **Relevance**: this is the single point every consumer already routes through. The orchestrator note (\"fix once where all callers route through\") points here.\n- **Reusable**: header-name case-insensitive lookup, and `__cause__`-chain walking bounded by `_MAX_CAUSE_CHAIN_DEPTH`.\n\n#### Pattern 4: PyGithub's own primary-limit backoff (upstream precedent)\n\n- **Location**: `.venv/lib/python3.13/site-packages/github/GithubRetry.py` (the `increment` override). The comments there read: \"we backoff primary rate limit at least until X-RateLimit-Reset\" and \"plus 1s as it is not clear when in that second the reset occurs\".\n- **Relevance**: the upstream library converts `x-ratelimit-reset` to `reset - now + 1` and ignores non-positive results. That is a direct precedent for the delay semantics AC2 asks for.\n- **Reusable**: the conversion semantics (epoch to delay, +1s skew margin, non-positive treated as no wait).\n\n### Existing Infrastructure\n\n- `SyncErrorKind` / `classify_sync_error` / `classify_github_failure`: `sync_state.py` is the single source of truth, and the classification table docstring sits at about lines 504-540.\n- The sync backoff already takes `max(_compute_backoff_delay(attempt), state.retry_after or 0.0)` (`sync_engine.py:259`). A server-supplied delay therefore already overrides the fixed schedule `_BACKOFF_DELAYS = (30.0, 120.0)` (`sync_engine.py:45`) with `MAX_RETRIES = 3` (`sync_engine.py:44`).\n- `make_github_client` builds `Github(auth=..., base_url=..., timeout=...)` with no `retry=` argument (`github_client.py:912`). PyGithub's `default_retry = GithubRetry()` therefore applies (`.venv/.../github/MainClass.py:169,187`). It includes 403 in `status_forcelist` and backs off primary-limit 403s until reset in-transport (see Q2).\n\n### Code References\n\n- `backlog_core/sync_state.py:351-379`: `_classify_github_exception`, the 403 branch that yields NON_RETRYABLE\n- `backlog_core/sync_state.py:412-455`: `parse_retry_after_header`, `retry_after_seconds` (Retry-After only)\n- `backlog_core/sync_engine.py:205-221,245-260`: OFFLINE decision and backoff delay\n- `backlog_core/server.py:308-323`: `_failure_verdict` (`retryable`, optional `retry_after`)\n- `backlog_core/gh_client.py:892-916,965-974`: `_is_rate_limited`, the `GitHubRateLimitedError` construction\n- `backlog_core/gh_client.py:2047-2048`: `probe_backend_status` maps any 403 to `RATE_LIMITED` independently. This is an existing inconsistency in the opposite direction (see Q6).\n- `.venv/.../github/Requester.py:943-988`: PyGithub `createException`, `isPrimaryRateLimitError`, `isSecondaryRateLimitError`\n\n---\n\n## Use Scenarios\n\n### Scenario 1: Agent hits the primary quota mid-grooming\n\n**Actor**: an agent running `/dh:work-backlog-item` through the backlog MCP tools\n**Trigger**: `backlog_groom` / `backlog_view` fails because the hourly REST quota is exhausted\n**Goal**: know whether to wait and retry or to abandon and report\n**Expected Outcome**: the error response says `retryable: true` with a `retry_after` roughly equal to the seconds until GitHub's quota resets. The agent can wait or defer instead of treating the backlog as broken.\n\n### Scenario 2: Background sync during a high-volume dispatch wave\n\n**Actor**: the background sync loop (observed via `sync_status`)\n**Trigger**: several parallel sessions exhaust the shared token's primary quota. The next sync attempt gets a primary-limit 403.\n**Goal**: sync recovers without anyone calling `sync_now`\n**Expected Outcome**: sync stays in its retry path and waits at least until the reset. It does not enter OFFLINE. `sync_status` shows a retrying state with a meaningful delay, not a terminal offline reason.\n\n### Scenario 3: Genuine permission-denied 403\n\n**Actor**: an agent or sync loop running with a token that lacks a scope\n**Trigger**: a 403 \"Resource not accessible by integration\" / \"Forbidden\" with no rate-limit signal\n**Goal**: fail fast with an actionable, permanent error\n**Expected Outcome**: still NON_RETRYABLE and still OFFLINE. No regression (AC5, AC7).\n\n### Scenario 4: GraphQL primary-limit refusal\n\n**Actor**: any caller of a GraphQL-backed operation (for example issue-status maps or bulk reads)\n**Trigger**: a GraphQL refusal with `x-ratelimit-remaining: 0`, `x-ratelimit-reset` set, and no `Retry-After`\n**Goal**: the same wait hint as the REST path\n**Expected Outcome**: `GitHubRateLimitedError.retry_after` is a positive, bounded delay derived from the reset time (AC9).\n\n### Scenario 5: Reset time already passed, or header garbage\n\n**Actor**: any consumer\n**Trigger**: `x-ratelimit-reset` is in the past (clock skew or slow handling), non-numeric, or missing\n**Goal**: no retry storm and no absurd sleep\n**Expected Outcome**: a safe delay (never negative, never unbounded). The retryable verdict is unaffected (AC2).\n\n---\n\n## Gap Analysis\n\n### Identified Gaps\n\n| # | Category | Gap Description | Impact |\n|---|----------|-----------------|--------|\n| 1 | Behavior | No stated upper bound on a reset-derived delay. The primary window is hourly, so a reset can be up to ~3600 s away, while `MAX_RETRIES = 3` and the fixed backoff tops out at 120 s. AC2 says \"bounded\" without a number. | Unbounded: the sync loop can sleep up to an hour per attempt. Too tight: the retry lands inside the same exhausted window and burns `MAX_RETRIES` to ERROR (Scenario 2). |\n| 2 | Behavior | PyGithub's default `GithubRetry` already sleeps until `x-ratelimit-reset` on a primary-limit 403 inside the transport, before any exception reaches the classifier (`github_client.py:912` uses the default retry). Nothing established under which conditions the `RateLimitExceededException` actually surfaces (retry total exhausted? GraphQL deadline? which call paths?). | Decides whether the fix changes observed behaviour on REST paths at all, how to reproduce it, and whether a synchronous MCP call can already block for up to an hour inside PyGithub (Scenario 1). |\n| 3 | Scope | Which signal defines \"primary limit\" for the classifier: `RateLimitExceededException` type (PyGithub maps both primary and secondary wording to it, `Requester.py:966-988`), the \"api rate limit exceeded\" message, `x-ratelimit-remaining == 0`, or a combination. | An over-broad signal flips ordinary permission 403s to retryable across 8+ call sites. An over-narrow one misses exceptions constructed without the subclass (AC1 is phrased by message/status, not by type). |\n| 4 | Scope | Primary-limit **429** responses are already RETRYABLE but get no delay without `Retry-After`. It is unclear whether the reset-derived delay applies to 429 as well as 403. | If excluded, a documented form of the same condition keeps retrying on the fixed 30/120 s schedule and may exhaust retries inside the window. |\n| 5 | Behavior | Precedence when both `Retry-After` and `x-ratelimit-reset` are present. The Change Frame says Retry-After first. GitHub's docs describe Retry-After for secondary limits only. | Low impact, but it should be stated so the tests pin it. |\n| 6 | Integration | `gh_client.probe_backend_status` (`gh_client.py:2047-2048`) maps **any** 403 to `RATE_LIMITED`. That is the opposite error from this item (permission 403s reported as rate limits). The Impact Radius excluded it. | Two different definitions of \"403 is a rate limit\" coexist. Status reporting and failure classification can disagree for the same response. |\n| 7 | Integration | Operator-visible change: the OFFLINE-then-`sync_now` recovery path fires less often for this cause, and a long reset wait shows up as a long \"retrying\" period. It is unknown whether anything documents or alerts on \"OFFLINE means rate limited\". | Low. This is the intended outcome, but `sync_status` output semantics change for rate-limited environments. |\n| 8 | Behavior | The `classify_sync_error` classification-table docstring (about `sync_state.py:504-540`) is the only design record of these rules. | It drifts unless it is updated in the same change. |\n\n---\n\n## Questions Requiring Resolution\n\n### Q1: Upper bound and margin for a reset-derived delay\n\n- **Resolution Channel**: architect-research\n- **Category**: Behavior\n- **Gap**: User proposed: \"take the delay from x-ratelimit-reset when Retry-After is absent.\" Research: consistent with GitHub's REST rate-limit docs (\"You should not retry your request until after the time specified by the x-ratelimit-reset header\") and with PyGithub's `GithubRetry` (`reset - now + 1`, ignoring non-positive). AC2 requires \"positive, bounded\" but gives no bound. The sync loop combines it via `max(backoff, retry_after)` (`sync_engine.py:259`) with `MAX_RETRIES = 3` (`:44`).\n- **Question**: Evaluate the bound, the clock-skew margin, and the past-reset behaviour for the converted delay. Determine whether the MCP `retry_after` and the sync-loop sleep should share one bound, given a window of up to one hour.\n- **Options**:\n  - A) No cap beyond \"time until reset + margin\". This matches GitHub's guidance.\n  - B) Cap at a fixed ceiling (for example the reset window), with retries continuing after.\n  - C) Report the full delay to MCP callers, but have the sync loop cap its single sleep and re-check.\n- **Why It Matters**: it decides between a retry storm inside an exhausted window and an hour-long stall (Gap 1).\n- **Resolution**: _pending_\n\n### Q2: What reaches the classifier, given PyGithub's in-transport primary-limit backoff?\n\n- **Resolution Channel**: architect-research\n- **Category**: Behavior\n- **Gap**: `make_github_client` (`github_client.py:912`) does not pass `retry=`, so PyGithub's `default_retry = GithubRetry()` applies (`MainClass.py:169,187`). `GithubRetry` retries 403s and sets its backoff to `x-ratelimit-reset - now + 1` for primary limits. The upstream code does not cap that by urllib3's `backoff_max`, because it replaces `get_backoff_time`. It was not established when a `RateLimitExceededException` escapes to `_classify_github_exception`: after urllib3 `total` retries are exhausted, on the GraphQL path bounded by `_call_with_deadline` (60 s default), or elsewhere.\n- **Question**: Trace the real call paths (REST via PyGithub objects, GraphQL via `requester.graphql_query` under `_call_with_deadline`) to find where a primary-limit refusal surfaces as an exception and how long the caller may already have blocked. Decide whether the in-transport retry is part of this item's scope or a separate finding.\n- **Why It Matters**: it determines test fixtures that reflect reality, whether the REST fix is observable, and whether MCP tool calls can hang for up to an hour inside PyGithub. That last point conflicts with #3968's \"stop GitHub requests hanging\" intent.\n- **Resolution**: _pending_\n\n### Q3: Which signal identifies a primary-limit 403\n\n- **Resolution Channel**: architect-research\n- **Category**: Scope\n- **Gap**: User proposed: \"classify RateLimitExceededException as retryable before the generic 403 rule.\" Research: viable and consistent with `gh_client._is_rate_limited` (`gh_client.py:907-908`). However, PyGithub raises `RateLimitExceededException` for **both** primary and secondary wording (`Requester.py:954-955,966-988`), and AC1 is phrased as \"a GithubException with status 403 \u2026 and a message indicating the primary quota is exhausted\", which a plain `GithubException` can satisfy without the subclass. `_is_rate_limited` also accepts `x-ratelimit-remaining == 0`. The existing negative tests (plain `GithubException(403, \"Forbidden\", {})`, GraphQL refusal) carry none of these signals.\n- **Question**: Evaluate whether the type check alone, the message, the exhausted-remaining header, or a union (mirroring `_is_rate_limited`) should define the retryable primary-limit 403 in `sync_state`, given the import-direction constraint.\n- **Why It Matters**: it sets how broad the verdict flip is across every consumer (Gap 3).\n- **Resolution**: _pending_\n\n### Q4: Does the reset-derived delay also cover 429 primary-limit responses?\n\n- **Resolution Channel**: architect-research\n- **Category**: Scope\n- **Gap**: GitHub documents primary-limit refusals as \"403 or 429\". `retry_after_seconds` inspects 403 and 429 for `Retry-After` only (`sync_state.py:450-454`). The item title names only 403.\n- **Question**: Evaluate whether the delay fallback applies uniformly to every rate-limit refusal status (consistent with `_RATE_LIMIT_REFUSAL_STATUSES`, `gh_client.py:106-107`) or only to 403.\n- **Why It Matters**: the same condition should produce the same wait hint whichever status GitHub picks.\n- **Resolution**: _pending_\n\n### Q5: Header precedence when both are present\n\n- **Resolution Channel**: architect-research\n- **Category**: Behavior\n- **Gap**: The Change Frame says Retry-After keeps precedence. GitHub describes `retry-after` for secondary limits and `x-ratelimit-reset` for primary limits.\n- **Question**: Confirm the precedence (Retry-After first, or the larger of the two) and pin it in tests.\n- **Why It Matters**: it prevents silent disagreement between the REST and GraphQL paths.\n- **Resolution**: _pending_\n\n### Q6: Is the `probe_backend_status` any-403-is-rate-limited rule in scope?\n\n- **Resolution Channel**: user-decision\n- **Category**: Integration\n- **Gap**: `gh_client.py:2047-2048` reports every 403 as `BackendAvailability.RATE_LIMITED`. The Impact Radius excluded it as off the call path. After this change the codebase will hold three differing definitions of a rate-limit 403 (`_is_rate_limited`, the classifier, `probe_backend_status`).\n- **Question**: Should aligning `probe_backend_status` with the same definition be part of this fix, or be filed as a separate backlog item? This is a scope call the item text does not settle.\n- **Why It Matters**: status output and error classification can contradict each other for the same response.\n- **Resolution**: _pending_. In auto mode the default is to keep it out of scope and record it as a follow-up.\n\n---\n\n## Goals (Pending Resolution)\n\n_These goals are finalized after the questions above are resolved._\n\n1. A GitHub refusal caused by exhausting the primary rate-limit quota is reported as temporary (retryable) on every path that classifies GitHub failures: background sync, MCP tool errors, and backend wrappers.\n2. That failure carries a wait hint that reflects GitHub's stated reset time when no `Retry-After` is sent, and it is identical in meaning on the REST and GraphQL paths.\n3. The wait hint is always safe: never negative, never unbounded, and robust to missing or malformed headers and to a reset time already in the past.\n4. Background sync recovers from a primary-limit refusal without manual intervention and without exhausting its retries inside the same exhausted window.\n5. Permission-denied 403s, environment-wide GraphQL refusals, and secondary-limit handling keep their current verdicts (AC5-AC8).\n6. The written record of the classification rules (the `classify_sync_error` table docstring) matches the behaviour.\n\n---\n\n## Risks\n\n- **Fan-out:** one classifier feeds 8+ call sites (`sync_engine.py`, `server.py`, `gh_client.py` x2, `backends/github_backend.py`). A mis-ordered or over-broad condition changes behaviour everywhere at once.\n- **Arithmetic:** epoch-to-delay conversion carries unit, sign, and clock-skew risk. There is no existing coverage.\n- **Unverified live shape:** this repo has no captured live primary-limit response. Header presence behind proxies is unconfirmed (Impact Radius \"Unknown frontier\").\n- **Hidden in-transport wait** (Q2) may mean the user-visible symptom on REST paths is a long block before any classification happens.\n\n---\n\n## Next Steps\n\n1. The design-spec agent resolves Q1-Q5 through research (starting points are cited in each question).\n2. Q6 defaults to out of scope in auto mode. Raise it as a follow-up backlog item unless the user decides otherwise.\n3. Finalize Goals, then RT-ICA, then architecture and task planning against the Impact Radius Systems Inventory.\n"}